\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Causal directed acyclic graph (DAG) for the drivers of observed IMF slope, discovered autonomously by \textsc  {Astra}\xspace  's PC\xspace  causal inference module from a synthetic 800-cluster dataset calibrated to \citet  {Steinhardt2026}, \citet  {Cohen2026}, and \citet  {Rosen2026}. Arrow directions indicate the inferred causal direction (Fisher $Z$ test, $\alpha = 0.05$). The density PDF slope ($\Sigma _{\mathrm  {PDF}}$) emerges as the primary causal driver ($\beta = 0.251$, $R^2 = 0.702$); Mach number acts on the IMF \emph  {through} $\Sigma _{\mathrm  {PDF}}$ rather than directly. Binary fraction introduces a systematic IMF slope bias of $\Delta \alpha = 0.045$, consistent with the `confidently wrong' range of \citet  {Rosen2026}. Metallicity does not directly drive IMF slope at $p < 0.05$, but approximately 23\,per\,cent of its marginal association with IMF slope is attributable to the Jeans mass pathway, supporting the causal chain of \citet  {Cohen2026}.}}{15}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:imf_causal_dag}{{9}{15}{Causal directed acyclic graph (DAG) for the drivers of observed IMF slope, discovered autonomously by \astra 's \pc causal inference module from a synthetic 800-cluster dataset calibrated to \citet {Steinhardt2026}, \citet {Cohen2026}, and \citet {Rosen2026}. Arrow directions indicate the inferred causal direction (Fisher $Z$ test, $\alpha = 0.05$). The density PDF slope ($\Sigma _{\mathrm {PDF}}$) emerges as the primary causal driver ($\beta = 0.251$, $R^2 = 0.702$); Mach number acts on the IMF \emph {through} $\Sigma _{\mathrm {PDF}}$ rather than directly. Binary fraction introduces a systematic IMF slope bias of $\Delta \alpha = 0.045$, consistent with the `confidently wrong' range of \citet {Rosen2026}. Metallicity does not directly drive IMF slope at $p < 0.05$, but approximately 23\,per\,cent of its marginal association with IMF slope is attributable to the Jeans mass pathway, supporting the causal chain of \citet {Cohen2026}}{figure.9}{}}
